  "notes": "The engineer-producer of hook-dense alt-pop, and the entry's signature is a hybrid rhythm bed rather than a pattern: \"**brushes plus a drum machine** layered together — a programmed 16th pattern under a brushed live kit\". That is why this model pairs a soft velocity tier (a brushed snare has no crack) with a pinned 16th hat at a high fill density — the machine underneath — and a live spread on the kit lanes only. 95–125 straight, backbeat on 2&4. Two more deltas are quoted: \"kick 1 and 3\" and \"tambourine on the off-8ths\" with `placement` \"offbeat\". The fourth fact is the one that empties the fill schedule: \"**wah, tremolo and flange applied to the drum bus as a transition device rather than using fills**\", so the fills sit at 16 bars and the hat rolls at 0.03 — the transitions are effects, not figures. Harmony is documented as three chords for a whole song: G–D–C, I–V–IV, \"no dominant resolution\", with a I–V–IV–I–ii bridge, so `maxChords` is 3 and one family carries most of the weight. The vocal architecture is \"rap-cadenced verses on 2–3 pitches against an anthemic sung chorus\" with **non-lexical hooks used as the chorus melody**, which is `motifPitchCount` 2–5 over a `call_response` structure at a wide density — the floor of 2 is the entry's own verse count. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability, `bpm.mode` 112 and the `motifPitchCount` ceiling of 5: the entry counts pitches for the verses only and gives the \"anthemic sung chorus\" no count; the key, progression, tempo, brushes-plus-machine method and the effects chain are sourced, the values are not.",
